---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Sources from "../components/Sources.astro";
import Plates from "../components/Plates.astro";
import Lightbox from "../components/Lightbox.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/pfullingen.json";
---
<Base
  title="Six Names at Pfullingen"
  eyebrow="Crikvenica → Württemberg, 1939–1949"
  dek="On 10 March 1947 the town of Pfullingen drew up a list of every foreigner registered there between the outbreak of war and the end of it. Under the heading Kroatien, six of the names are Defranceski, and five of the six were born at Crikvenica.">

  <section class="prose">
    <p>The list is a bureaucratic object — a table of names, birth dates and birthplaces, typed to satisfy an Allied inquiry two years after the fact. But read down the birthplace column and it becomes something else: a village on the Kvarner coast, reassembled a thousand kilometres inland, in a small Swabian town below the Alb.</p>
    <p>They were not deported together and they did not all come for the same reason. Petar left in April 1939, five months before the war, of his own accord and to qualify — he was a ship's engineer at Split and wanted the German papers. Ivan entered work in October 1941 at sixteen, six months after Yugoslavia was partitioned. Zdravko's route is the one that stops you: a clerk pencilled it in the margin of his sickness-fund card, and it says he was taken by the Partisans in 1943, held three or four months, and then went straight to Germany.</p>
    <p>What they did there was, almost all of them, the same thing. Motor mechanics. Three of the men worked for the same employer, <strong>Hermann Sauer</strong> of Reutlingen and Pfullingen — a firm repairing vehicles through the war years. On the French camp cards after it, under <em>Profession</em>, the word recurs: <span class="mono">auto-mécanicien</span>.</p>
  </section>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:44px">The six, and the two wives</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>Birthplace</th><th>Trade</th><th>What the file says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{d.people.map((p) => (
        <tr style={p.status === "withheld" ? "opacity:.72" : undefined}>
          <td>{p.name}</td><td class="m">{p.born}</td><td>{p.birthplace}</td>
          <td>{p.trade}</td><td>{p.role}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">Three faces</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">The <em>Fiche Individuelle</em> of the French occupation zone carried a clipped passport photograph and five fingerprints in red ink. These are the only likenesses of the Crikvenica family the archive holds.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px"><Plates group="dp" /></div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">Ten years, in the order the paper falls</h2>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">When</th><th>What</th><th>Around it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{d.chronology.map((c) => (
        <tr><td class="m">{c.y}</td><td>{c.t}</td><td>{c.w}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">{d.residence.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">{d.residence.intro}</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Born</th><th class="m">Arrived</th><th class="m">Left</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{d.residence.rows.map(r => (
        <tr><td>{r.who}</td><td class="m">{r.born}</td><td class="m">{r.from}</td><td class="m">{r.to}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">{d.residence.read.split("\n\n").map(p => <p>{p}</p>)}</div>
  <div style="margin-top:20px"><Plates ids={["pfullingen-legible"]} cols={1} /></div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">{d.neuhausen.heading}</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">{d.neuhausen.intro}</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:18px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="m">№</th><th>Name</th><th class="m">Born</th><th class="m">Present</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{d.neuhausen.rows.map(r => (
        <tr><td class="m">{r.n}</td><td>{r.who}</td><td class="m">{r.born}</td><td class="m">{r.present}</td><td>{r.note || "—"}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div style="margin-top:20px"><Plates ids={["neuhausen-list"]} cols={1} /></div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">{d.neuhausen.read.split("\n\n").map(p => <p>{p}</p>)}</div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">Found in 2026, and not in the family's file</h2>
  <p class="dek" style="margin-top:8px">The Arolsen Archives hold 79 records under this surname. The family's own copy of file D-1.505 is a fraction of it.</p>
  <div style="margin-top:20px"><Plates ids={["reutlingen-list","petar-counselling","petar-fiche-colour","felix-card-colour"]} /></div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p><strong>And they were not alone.</strong> The Reutlingen town list puts Felix in the town from 15 August 1941 to 8 October 1942 — but look at the names bracketing his. Brozicevic, born Crikvenica 1910, present from March 1939. Car Pavao, born Crikvenica 1911, from February 1939. Anna, Maria and Zdravko Domijan, all born at Crikvenica, from January and June 1939. Dracic Ivan, born Crikvenica 1912, from <strong>17 October 1938</strong> to January 1946.</p>
    <p>There was a Crikvenica colony at Reutlingen <em>before the war began</em>. That is why Petar could leave Split in April 1939 and say he was going to improve himself: he was not striking out into nowhere. He was going where people from his own town already were.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">The paper itself</h2>
  <div style="margin-top:20px"><Plates group="documents" /></div>

  <h2 style="font-size:28px;margin-top:48px">Where they actually went</h2>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:16px">
    <p>The forms end in 1950 and the paper stops. The graves pick it up.</p>
    <p>Zdravko, Ruža and their son did not go to America. They went to <strong>Australia</strong> — to <strong>Horsham</strong>, a wheat town in the Wimmera of western Victoria, four hundred kilometres inland from Melbourne and about as far from the Kvarner as a coast-born mechanic could get. Zdravko died there on <strong>22 December 1979</strong>, aged fifty-eight, and the headstone calls him <strong>Frank</strong>. Ruža followed on <strong>1 February 1996</strong> and lies in the same ground.</p>
    <p>Their son <strong>Anton</strong> — the <em>enfant</em> of the camp card, the toddler in Ruža's photograph — died on <strong>28 June 1999</strong> and is buried at Warrill Park, Willowbank, in the City of Ipswich. That is in <strong>Queensland</strong>, an hour up the highway from Brisbane, where the keeper of this archive lives. Nobody in this family knew.</p>
    <p><a href={u("/graves/")}>The grave gazetteer →</a></p>
  </div>

  <section class="prose" style="margin-top:44px">
    <h3>And Petar stayed</h3>
    <p><strong>He never went.</strong> A counselling card found in the Arolsen collections in 2026 — not in the file the family holds — carries three lines that end the story. On <strong>22 July 1949</strong>: <em>«Veut rester en Allemagne, a une maison, 2 autos etc.»</em> — wants to stay in Germany, has a house, two cars. On <strong>28 April 1951</strong> he confirmed that the family wished to remain. On <strong>25 May 1951</strong> they were entered on the list of displaced persons living privately. The address is the same one he gave in 1947: Kelternstraße 30, Neuhausen.</p>
    <p>The ship's engineer who left Split in April 1939 with nothing had, ten years later, a house and two cars in a Swabian village, and decided that was enough. His two daughters, born at Urach in 1940 and Metzingen in 1945, grew up German.</p>
    <p>Petar had asked for the United States, and named California, because his aunt <strong>Jacomina Alafetić</strong> was at San Pedro — the fishing and cannery port south of Los Angeles that drew Dalmatian and Kvarner families for two generations. Ivan went the other way: back to the coast, to Senj, and the line this archive follows runs on through him. See <a href={u("/direct-line/")}>The Spine</a>.</p>
    <h3>A discrepancy worth naming</h3>
    <p>The family records Anton, born 1890, as dying in 1943. Yet a certificate about his wartime employment was issued by the mayor of Pfullingen on 28 February 1950. Certificates of that kind were routinely obtained after a man's death, by a son making a claim, so the two need not conflict — but nothing here proves it either way, and it is left open.</p>
    <h3>On dates that do not agree</h3>
    <p>Three of the six have two birth dates in these files: Ivan 24 or 25 June 1925, Zdravko 1 or 2 April 1921, Ruža 13 February or 2 May 1922. Nothing sinister — a town clerk copying from a list, a camp officer copying from a man's memory in a second language. Both forms are shown rather than one silently chosen.</p>
    <h3>Source</h3>
    <p>All the documents on this page come from the <strong>Arolsen Archives</strong>, the former International Tracing Service at Bad Arolsen, holder of the Nazi-era and displaced-persons records of some seventeen and a half million people. The file for this family is numbered <span class="mono">ITS D-1.505</span>. The copies were obtained by the family and are reproduced here from their own scans.</p>
  </section>

  <Lightbox />
  <Sources ids={["arolsen", "findagrave", "mftpkg"]} />
</Base>
